<Article title="Visible eSIM" description="Moving iPhone activation into the app, with a cross-functional team at Visible / Verizon." canonicalPath="/case-studies/visible-esim.html" eyebrow="From the archive · 2018–2021">
<p class="archive-note">A condensed account from my previous portfolio, retained at its original address. The expanded case study is taking shape.</p>
<h2>My role</h2>
<p>I was the design lead on a cross-functional team with product management and engineering. I worked with Apple, Verizon, and Visible engineers on an activation experience that used eSIM to set up service within the app.</p>
<h2>The opportunity</h2>
<p>A physical SIM kit introduced a gap between signing up and getting connected. eSIM offered a way to bring that experience into the digital product, without waiting for a card to arrive.</p>
<h2>Understanding activation</h2>
<p>Diary studies helped us understand what happened after a kit reached someone’s home. Some people put it aside; others were unsure whether the SIM was already installed or what to do next. Instructions seen during shopping were not always remembered at activation.</p>
<p>Those findings shifted attention from individual screens to the whole experience of getting connected: what people expected, what they understood, and what happened away from the app.</p>
<h2>Bringing setup into the app</h2>
<p>The eSIM flow connected device compatibility, setup, and activation in one app-based experience. The intent was to make the next step clear and remove the physical handoff from the journey.</p>
<h2>Independent coverage</h2>
<blockquote><p>“The most streamlined installation (and most impressive) I have experienced is on Visible.”</p></blockquote>
<p>Lynnette Luna, GlobalData analyst, quoted by Linda Hardesty in <a href="https://www.fierce-network.com/wireless/big-u-s-carriers-now-support-esim-but-they-re-not-marketing-it">Fierce Wireless, June 9, 2021</a>. This describes the activation experience at that time.</p>
